WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.140
Thousands of U.S. passengers were stranded Friday when failing routers halted every takeoff. This is Omni View North America, episode 47. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. After that, Canada's auto talks with Washington, a Medicaid deadline pushed into summer, and the last uncalled Senate race, then a short run of other news, and a union contract in British Columbia. The Federal Aviation Administration ordered that rare nationwide halt on Friday afternoon. The New York Times reported,

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.680
and NPR covered it, too, once old air traffic gear overloaded with data and started failing.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.120
Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy told reporters at the White House that night,

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.800
the radar and the rest of the communications began to stumble at 336 in the afternoon,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.520
when those routers filled up. The failures were not everywhere, but they were wide enough

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.900
that take-offs stopped. The Times counted the stranded in the thousands and said airlines

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.340
and airports were still trying to recover. NPR's figure was millions, and it said no aircraft

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.720
were departing any airport.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.020
Homeland Security Secretary Mark Wayne Mullen said investigators had seen zero evidence this

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:05.900
was a cyber attack.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.620
He suggested newer gear and technology Duffy's department had bolted onto the old network

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.000
were what overwhelmed it.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.060
Duffy's account was the opposite, antiquated legacy equipment, not the recent upgrades.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.960
In another telling, Mullen called the stop a telecommunication outage and said he did

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.080
not know what caused it.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.740
At the White House briefing NPR sat in on.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.240
He said the United States was not under a threat.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.540
Duffy also said controllers could still reach pilots while the outage came and went.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.320
On the radio, a controller at Newark Liberty passed the stop to Cruz.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.680
Minutes later, controllers in Atlanta and Chicago said they had little information.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.420
As arrivals kept coming and departure sat at the gates, the talk turned to gate trouble

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.320
and a tarmac that felt like a parking lot.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.360
One pilot, speaking to Newark, noted the date.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.620
It was Friday the 13th, and that was not lost on him.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.320
Administration officials use the failure to demand that lawmakers approve tens of billions

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.400
of dollars more, so system-wide upgrades move faster.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:07.080
The previous nationwide ground stop in 2023 followed a pilot warning system going down

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.460
and ran about 90 minutes.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.640
If a flight was canceled, the traveler is owed a refund no matter the reason.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.620
A hotel room or a meal voucher is not something the airlines will guarantee.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.660
Some pro and college teams were stuck on runways or shifting onto buses, with weekend

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.620
games now uncertain.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:29.520
Airports filled with families who could not leave, and passengers on the tarmac told NPR they were sitting there without information.

